  ('zcrm_1579374000130189008', 'contact', 'zcrm_1579374000130311002', 'Nicole Plourde', NULL, '12-14-23 Response:
Good Morning Nikki,

See below, I''ve added my answers among your questions below in green highlight.  I am arriving back from my mini-road trip (1400+/- miles!) this evening and would be happy to discuss any of the below on the phone. I am flexible and can even speak in the evening when kids are asleep today, tomorrow, even over the weekend. 

Best,

Kirk
I had a chance to go over the guidelines again (without kids interrupting, can you believe it?!) and write the rest of the burning questions I have.
What''s important to me:
1. sick visits- not telehealth, in person visits. I need these at least partially covered. 
The only membership we have where this is provided is with the Secure CoPay membership: With those office visits there is a flat co-pay charged. For example, standard office visit copay is $20 and a specialist would be $50. Unlimited visits. BUT, with the Secure memberships (both Secure HSA and Secure CoPay) you will need to be self-employed. And if you cannot go the self-employment route, then that leaves Care+ which does not cover any office visits.
2. preventative care- if not 100%, a portion needs to be covered- it is the foundation for retaining health.
I agree with your thinking! With Care+, this is not provided for.  With one of the two Secure memberships, it is. But then, you have the self-employment hurdle to overcome.
3. labs- covered if ordered by provider related to medical need or partially OR discounted if requested per person.
If lab is ordered as part of an annual wellness checkup, you would need to discuss this with the doctor and make sure they code this as "preventative", then it is covered. BUT, the moment they code something as "diagnostic" or anything other than preventative, you will be charged for it. 
4. specialties- discounted copay?? how is this handled? With Secure CoPay as mentioned above, the fees are disclosed defined as $50 for a specialty visit. (Ie, dermatologist, urologist, OB/gynecologist, etc.)

The biggest question that''s important to our family is the pre-membership conditions- for example. You know Dylan (my youngest) has asthma. Now lets say he goes to the emergency room and their diagnoses is asthma exacerbation. Will that be covered? Because, as we know, it will definitely exceed any IUA. If Dylan has an attack that warrants a visit to the emergency room AND this is a pre-existing condition, there would be $0 shared in year 1 of the membership per page 18-19 of the guidelines. I think I''ve shown this doc to you before, let me know if not. 

I really think the above ought to do it for peace of mind! Thanks for being so responsive and supportive. The need for sharing is so individualized, and you do a great job guiding the journey. 

Hope you are enjoying vacation,
Nikki and boys', 'Kirk Bennett', 'Kirk Bennett', '2023-12-14 08:15:22', '2023-12-14 08:15:22'),
