  ('zcrm_1579374000125775003', 'contact', 'zcrm_1579374000013590042', 'Donald J. Hinz Jr.', NULL, '09.28.2023 CARI DOES NOT HAVE ACCESS TO THE CRM PRESENTLY. HERE ARE HER NOTES:
9-28-2023:  Donald e-mailed that as he''s calling around asking about doctor''s appts, he''s being told no one takes ''Planstin'' & was he telling the wrong thing - 
VIA OUTLOOK
Hi Donald,
Planstin is a ''TPA'' (Third Party Administrator) for the qualified ACA (Affordable Care Act) MEC (Minimal Essential Coverage) plan.
For Preventative Care items only, I would recommend presenting your Planstin card.  On the card is Planstin''s phone number and they can help the doctor''s office understand what is required for you to get your ''preventative care'' appointment paid for.  It really comes down to either:
     - a PHCS network doctor will have a contract & be in network
OR
     - an out-of-network doctor is willing to bill Planstin per their instructions
        when they call the number on the card.
EITHER WAY: your covered ''preventative care'', when billed directly to Planstin at no charge to you.  It just must be on the list of covered ACA MEC covered benefits (here''s the website for ''all adults'' coverages):
Preventive care benefits for adults | HealthCare.gov
Remember to keep this appointment purely ACA MEC ''preventative care'' items, otherwise it can change the way the appointment is medically coded and that may mean it moves outside of a covered medical code.
NOTE:  During your annual ''preventative care'' appointment, many doctors will want to order lab work.  This is something we must proactively work on, getting you a discount.  The ACA MEC pays very little regarding lab work.  Please call us here on the concierge line before you get this done and we will walk you through the options.
Respectfully yours,
Cari Adams-Waneka
MPB Health Concierge
1-800-519-2969', 'Nahla Tebcherany', 'Nahla Tebcherany', '2023-09-29 10:14:28', '2023-09-29 10:17:01'),
